{
    "compilerOptions": {
        "target": "es2019",
        "lib": ["dom", "dom.iterable", "es2020"],
        "allowJs": true,
        "jsx": "react-jsx",
        "module": "esnext",
        "moduleResolution": "bundler",
        "noImplicitAny": true,
        "strict": true,
        "esModuleInterop": true,
        "skipLibCheck": true,
        "forceConsistentCasingInFileNames": true,
        "verbatimModuleSyntax": true,
        "types": ["jest", "node"]
    },
    "include": ["src/**/*"]
}
// This file is used to configure the TypeScript compiler options for the project.
// It specifies the target ECMAScript version, the libraries to include, and other compiler options.
// The "include" option specifies the files to include in the compilation process.
// The "allowJs" option allows JavaScript files to be compiled along with TypeScript files.
// The "jsx" option specifies the JSX code generation mode.
// "module": "esnext" + "moduleResolution": "bundler" match how webpack resolves imports (TypeScript 6+ deprecates "node").
// "types" lists the global typings explicitly (TypeScript 6+ no longer loads every @types package): Jest globals and Node (util, used by jest.setup.ts).
// "verbatimModuleSyntax" requires `import type` for type-only imports, so Babel removes them safely.
// TypeScript only type-checks here (npm run typecheck): Babel and webpack emit the bundle, so there is no outDir.
// The "esModuleInterop" option enables emit interoperability between CommonJS and ES Modules.
// The "skipLibCheck" option skips type checking of declaration files.
// The "forceConsistentCasingInFileNames" option ensures that file names are case-sensitive.
// The "lib" option specifies the library files to include in the compilation process.
// The "target" option specifies the ECMAScript version to compile to.
